 "notes": [
  "Regular-season records parsed by script from the English Wikipedia standings templates (Template:<year> WNBA Eastern/Western Conference standings for 1997-2019, Template:<year> WNBA standings for 2020-2026); champions parsed from the table in the 'WNBA Finals' article. Retrieved 2026-11-16.",
  "Seasons covered: 1997-2026 (30 seasons). The 1997 title was a single championship game. 2020 was a shortened 22-game bubble season.",
  "15 teams played in 2026. The 15th team, missing from the 14-team feed list, is the Connecticut Sun (11-33), who played 2026 in Uncasville under that name.",
  "PENDING CHANGE FOR 2027: the Sun were sold to the Fertitta family (WNBA/NBA Boards of Governors approval 2026-05-13) and move to Houston as the 'Houston Comets' from the 2027 season. This file still keys the franchise as 'Connecticut Sun' (metro Hartford) because that is the name it carried in every finished season and in the 2026 standings. When the team feed switches, rename the key 'Connecticut Sun' to 'Houston Comets' everywhere, set its metro to Houston and append [2027, \"Houston Comets\", \"Houston\"] to its eras.",
  "The original Houston Comets (1997-2008, champions 1997-2000) folded in December 2008 and are keyed 'defunct:Houston Comets'. Per Wikipedia it is the Miracle/Sun franchise history, not the original Comets record, that follows the relocated club to Houston, so the two are kept separate; re-check the league's official record book before the 2027 season in case the WNBA assigns the 1997-2008 history to the new Comets.",
  "Portland Fire: the 2000-2002 team folded and is keyed 'defunct:Portland Fire'. The 2026 Portland Fire is a new expansion franchise (awarded 2024) that revived the name only; it has just the 2026 season.",
  "Lineages followed: Utah Starzz (1997-2002) -> San Antonio Silver Stars (2003-2013) -> San Antonio Stars (2014-2017) -> Las Vegas Aces (2018-); Detroit Shock (1998-2009) -> Tulsa Shock (2010-2015) -> Dallas Wings (2016-); Orlando Miracle (1999-2002) -> Connecticut Sun (2003-2026).",
  "Defunct franchises: Houston Comets (1997-2008), Sacramento Monarchs (1997-2009), Charlotte Sting (1997-2006), Cleveland Rockers (1997-2003), Miami Sol (2000-2002), Portland Fire (2000-2002).",
  "Metros: Connecticut Sun (Uncasville) use 'Hartford'; Golden State Valkyries 'SF Bay Area'; Indiana Fever 'Indianapolis'. 'Tulsa' is the only metro not in the existing list and is defined in 'metros'.",
  "Clubs under \"inactive\" have folded but keep their history: they can be picked and ranked, and their cities are credited. Each lists the \"defunct:\" names it covers in \"names\" (its own name when left out)."
